    <p id="d1e572">The saturation anomaly of a gas is the percent
deviation of the measured concentration relative to the expected
concentration of the sample in equilibrium with air at one atmosphere, for
example, as defined by <span class="inline-formula"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" id="M16" display="inline" overflow="scroll" dspmath="mathml"><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow class="chem"><mi mathvariant="normal">He</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn mathvariant="normal">100</mn><mo>(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>-</mo><mn mathvariant="normal">1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></math><span><svg xmlns:svg="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="105pt" height="14pt" class="hide-js svg-formula" dspmath="mathimg" md5hash="d547568870bb5e299482876d22a7d1ad"><image xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/11/441/2019/essd-11-441-2019-ie00001.svg" width="100%" height="14pt" src="https://essd.copernicus.org/articles/11/441/2019/essd-11-441-2019-ie00001.png"></image></svg></span></span>, where
<span class="inline-formula"><i>C</i><sup>∗</sup></span> is the solubility equilibrium concentration at the temperature and
salinity of the sample.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fn" id="fn_Ch1.Footn2">
    <span class="note-mark">
